<interrogationes unitas="exemplaria">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I test whether a pixel of a classic Mac 8x8 pattern is painted?
    <responsum symbolum="exemplar_punctum exemplar_obtinere">Call
      exemplar_punctum(id, x, y): it tiles the pattern over the whole
      plane (x and y are taken modulo VIII with &amp; VII, so negative
      coordinates wrap too: -1 is column 7) and returns VERUM where the
      pattern paints. To get the raw rows instead, exemplar_obtinere(id)
      returns the eight bytes.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the bit layout of the eight bytes exemplar_obtinere returns?
    <responsum symbolum="exemplar_obtinere exemplar_punctum">One
      byte per row, row 0 first; inside a byte the HIGH bit (0x80) is
      x 0 and the low bit is x 7. A set bit means painted (the
      foreground colour). exemplar_punctum reads exactly
      e[y &amp; VII] &amp; (I &lt;&lt; (VII - (x &amp; VII))).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass any number as a pattern id?
    <responsum symbolum="exemplar_obtinere exemplar_punctum exemplar_internum_t">Yes,
      it is checked: an id at or above EXEMPLAR_NUMERUS (38 patterns,
      0 to 37) makes exemplar_obtinere return NIHIL and exemplar_punctum
      return FALSUM (so a bad id paints nothing rather than crashing).
      The id is i32, which is unsigned, so there is no negative case.
      Callers that memcpy the bytes must check for NIHIL first.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>which pattern ids are solid black and plain white?
    <responsum symbolum="exemplar_internum_t exemplar_obtinere">EXEMPLAR_SOLIDUS
      (0) has every bit set, so everything is painted; EXEMPLAR_ALBUS
      (19) has none set. They are not at the two ends of the table:
      the order follows the classic Mac pattern palette, not
      darkness.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why is the pattern table its own library instead of living in delineare?
    <responsum symbolum="exemplar_obtinere exemplar_punctum" via="lib/exemplaria.worklog.md">It
      was moved verbatim out of lib/delineare.c on 2026-10-09 so the
      command rasterizers (the vicus focus indicator) could use the
      same patterns without the legacy painter. delineare still reads
      it through exemplar_obtinere. Adding a library the legacy painter
      uses means updating three hand-written or generated build lists
      (ludus_tessera and tessera compile_probationes.sh,
      tessera/musivum_fontes_generata.sh).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
